A||||14||isthiswhereiputausername?||extra way to get into the trunk?||||5||
Z||000000||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-21-2001||02:04 PM||||Thinking of filling over my trunk lock and did anyone successfully hook up another alternate way of getting into the trunk if you have a dead battery and cant use the power trunk?(just in case)<P>(without relocating the battery)<P>maybe a hidden cable or something?<P><p>[This message has been edited by isthiswhereiputausername? (edited 07-21-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||07-21-2001||02:48 PM||||I only have the electric release, since I don't have the right trunk key.<BR>When my battery went dead. (interior lights left on for 3 days) I had to run some jumper wires from another battery to the cigarette lighter plug. Outer part is -, inner is +.<BR>It gets enough current flow through the electrical system to let you use the electric trunk release. Then you can jump start the battery after that.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||KRMFiero||07-21-2001||02:50 PM||||Well when I cant find my keys and battery is still good I climb under car and take a wire from + batery to the trunk relese wire to open the trunk...    so maybe run some wires to hook up like a extra battery ex. jump it that you can just pull down to hook up or something hmmm not sure, a cable would be kinda hard i would think....||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroBUZZ||07-21-2001||02:52 PM||||I am in the midst of a similar mod.  I plan to have the trunk lid (only over the actual trunk) hinge backwards.  I am using a hood release with a T handle cable to actuate it.  The catch will be on the car and the D bar on the trunk (opposite to stock). I don't see why a generic pull cable couldn't be adapted to fit the stock release along with the electrics.<BR>Gary<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000004||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-21-2001||03:31 PM||||thanks for the info guys  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I was actually thinking of adding an extra set of wires from my power trunk up to my front compartment that i can hook a current into if I need to open it if I'm ever stuck..<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||maryjane||07-21-2001||03:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pontiaddict:<BR><B>I only have the electric release, since I don't have the right trunk key.<BR>When my battery went dead. (interior lights left on for 3 days) I had to run some jumper wires from another battery to the cigarette lighter plug. Outer part is -, inner is +.<BR>It gets enough current flow through the electrical system to let you use the electric trunk release. Then you can jump start the battery after that.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's pretty ingenious, I'd never have thought of it. Guess the key has to be on?<BR>Any way it could damage anything else?||||reg||1
Z||000006||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-21-2001||03:46 PM||||wouldnt damage anything..some of the portable boosting devices connect through the cig lighter||||reg||1
Z||000007||JayUSA77||07-21-2001||04:06 PM||||I know this doesnt really have to do with your subject but i thought i would tell you guys about a good product thats out.  Its called a power booster and all it is is a little carry bag made of plastic that weighs around 25 pounds and has the wires to hook up your battery to it.  When my battery dies i hook that thing up and my car starts right up.  Just thought id tell you its a good idea since you dont have to wait around for someone to jump you.  <BR>J<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||Pontiaddict||07-21-2001||09:24 PM||||With my luck, I'd keep it in the trunk.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I actually got the idea for popping my trunk from one of those cigarette lighter battery boosting contraptions. The wires running to the cig lighter arent heavy enought to handle major current but you don't need much to pop the trunk. You might manage to do it with a 9 volt or one of those lantern batteries.<P>Maryjane, You'd only need the key to be on if it's required to pop the trunk. I've heard that earlier Fieros needed the key to be on, but my 86GT only works when the key is off, or the e-brake is on.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||07-21-2001||09:32 PM||||If you've lost your key or don't have the correct one (or even if you just want to) you can go down to your local GM dealer with you VIN and registration and they can look up the key code in their data base and cut you a brand new key.||||reg||1
Z||000010||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-21-2001||11:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>If you've lost your key or don't have the correct one (or even if you just want to) you can go down to your local GM dealer with you VIN and registration and they can look up the key code in their data base and cut you a brand new key.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Thanks for the info  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||lowCG||07-22-2001||01:21 AM||||I tried the dealer routine back a couple months when my car was in the body shop with a dead battery(same problem of no trunk access)with no luck.The bodyshop idiots wouldn,t/couldn't figure out how to power it up,so I had to pay a locksmith to drill out the cylinder.<BR>If you find a way to set it up with a cable,do please let us know,as I'd like to fill the hole left in my trunk.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Songman||07-22-2001||03:04 AM||||I have one of the cigarette lighter booster setups. Lots less bulky to carry around in a Fiero! But of course, you'll always run up on the car that the cigarette lighter don't work.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroBUZZ||07-22-2001||11:49 AM||||After some thought, I see my Aerosnore van has those little bullets in the door jamb to pass current for the electric gee-haws.  If you ran a second wire from your release to one of these hidden somewhere you could easily touch it with a hot wire, it should pop.<BR>Gary<BR>PS I'm on my way to Cape Breton this week and may have some room in a small UHaul, if anyone down east needs some parts. I can leave them in Sackville, NB or along the way.||||reg||7
Z||000014||Joe Torma||07-22-2001||01:39 PM||||On the trunk solinoid latch thing, there is a slot that the lock turns to release it.  Right now I have to open my trunk with a screwdriver...The inside metal that holds my lock is all rusted out so I'm going to make a lever that fits into the slot and attach a cable to it as a backup.<P>Joe||||reg||1
